{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-i2-reviewer-fable-42","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:5f4ec238d04074266e32","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"For the selected result in results/report.md (Welch's two-sample t-test on 48 aliquots, t = 12.84, df = 45.0, p < 0.0001), the workflow enters all four repeated aliquot rows from each of the 12 starter jars into scipy.stats.ttest_ind as if each row were an independent observation, reporting n = 24 per flour group and df = 45.0, even though the data description designates jar_id as the independent unit and states the four aliquots per jar are measurements of the same batch; there are only 6 independent jars per group, so the reported degrees of freedom and precision are inflated roughly fourfold relative to a jar-level analysis.","root_cause":"read_groups() in workflow/analysis.py keys the data only by flour_type and appends every row's acidity_ml to a flat per-group list, never reading or aggregating over jar_id; these pooled lists of 24 values per group (4 correlated aliquots x 6 jars) are then passed directly to stats.ttest_ind, a row-independent procedure, so within-jar repeated measurements are treated as independent replicates.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description names jar_id as the independent unit and defines a row as a single aliquot titration, so rows within a jar are not independent units.","observed_value":"Independent unit column: jar_id; one row = one titration of one aliquot from one jar","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":22,"end_line":23,"quoted_text":"One row is: one titration of one aliquot drawn from one starter jar\nIndependent unit column: jar_id"}]},{"description":"The description explicitly states the four aliquots per jar are repeated measurements of the same batch, not separate batches, so they carry within-jar dependence.","observed_value":"4 aliquots per jar are measurements of the same batch","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":8,"end_line":11,"quoted_text":"At the end of the 24 h window a technician drew four separate aliquots out of each\njar and titrated every aliquot with 0.1 M NaOH to the phenolphthalein endpoint,\nwriting down the volume of base in millilitres. The four aliquots taken from one jar\nare four measurements of the same batch of starter, not four separate batches."}]},{"description":"read_groups() pools every row's acidity_ml into a flat list keyed only by flour_type; jar_id is never read, so repeated aliquots from the same jar enter the pooled sample as separate observations.","observed_value":"groups keyed by flour_type only; jar_id ignored","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":18,"end_line":24,"quoted_text":"def read_groups():\n    \"\"\"Return {flour_type: [acidity_ml, ...]} from the titration sheet.\"\"\"\n    groups = {}\n    with INPUT_PATH.open(newline=\"\", encoding=\"ascii\") as handle:\n        for row in csv.DictReader(handle):\n            groups.setdefault(row[\"flour_type\"], []).append(float(row[\"acidity_ml\"]))\n    return groups"}]},{"description":"The pooled per-aliquot lists are passed directly to scipy.stats.ttest_ind, a procedure whose validity assumes each entry is an independent observation.","observed_value":"ttest_ind applied to 24 rye and 24 wheat aliquot values","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":47,"end_line":47,"quoted_text":"outcome = stats.ttest_ind(rye, wheat, equal_var=False)"}]},{"description":"The input data contain four rows per jar (shown here for jar R01), confirming repeated entries per independent unit flow into the pooled samples.","observed_value":"4 rows for jar R01 (aliquots 1-4)","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":5,"quoted_text":"R01,rye,14,1,9.5\nR01,rye,14,2,9.1\nR01,rye,14,3,9.7\nR01,rye,14,4,9.3"}]},{"description":"The report states per-group sample sizes of 24 aliquots and the selected result claims df = 45.0 on 48 aliquots, reflecting the aliquot-level (not jar-level) sample size; only 6 independent jars exist per group.","observed_value":"n = 24 per group, df = 45.0, 48 aliquots","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":10,"end_line":11,"quoted_text":"| Rye | 24 | 10.000 | 0.404 |\n| Wheat | 24 | 8.600 | 0.349 |"},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":22,"end_line":22,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Welch's two-sample t-test on 48 aliquots shows that rye-refreshed starters reach a higher titratable acidity than wheat-refreshed starters, by 1.400 mL of 0.1 M NaOH (t = 12.84, df = 45.0, p < 0.0001)."}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The report transparently labels its counts as aliquots rather than claiming 48 independent starters, and a jar-level reanalysis (jar means: rye 9.4, 9.8, 10.0, 10.2, 10.4, 10.2 vs wheat 8.2, 8.4, 8.6, 8.6, 8.8, 9.0; Welch t ~ 7.5 on ~10 df) would still be highly significant, so the direction of the substantive conclusion is not an artifact of the pooling. However, this does not negate the issue: the selected result makes a jar-level substantive claim about starters while its reported t, df, SDs, and sample sizes are computed by entering the 4 correlated aliquot rows per jar into ttest_ind as independent observations.","observed_value":"conclusion direction robust to jar-level aggregation; reported statistics are not","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":15,"end_line":16,"quoted_text":"Welch's two-sample t-test (two-sided, unequal variances) comparing the acidity of every\nrye aliquot with the acidity of every wheat aliquot."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":5,"end_line":6,"quoted_text":"48 aliquots were titrated after a 24 h refresh; the response is titratable acidity in\nmL of 0.1 M NaOH to the phenolphthalein endpoint."}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I attempted to falsify the issue three ways. (1) I checked whether the workflow aggregates to one value per jar before testing: it does not; read_groups() never reads jar_id and appends every row's acidity_ml directly, and no other function touches jar_id. (2) I checked whether aliquots could legitimately be treated as independent units: the data description rules this out explicitly ('four measurements of the same batch of starter, not four separate batches') and names jar_id as the independent unit column. (3) I checked whether the selected result confines its claim to aliquot-level description only: it does not; it asserts that 'rye-refreshed starters reach a higher titratable acidity than wheat-refreshed starters' — a claim about jars/starters — while citing df = 45.0 derived from treating 48 correlated aliquot rows as independent (correct jar-level df would be ~10 with 6 jars per group). I also verified arithmetically from data/input.csv that the reported group means (10.000, 8.600), n = 24 per group, and the fourfold inflation of per-group sample size (24 aliquots vs 6 jars) are consistent with pooling all rows. All falsification attempts failed, so the issue stands as demonstrated.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}